.bd
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
pass0
- C preprocessor and lexer


.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
pass0 [options] source.c


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Pass0
is the first pass of the C compiler.  It is not the C preprocessor of
a Unix and does not write preprocessed C: it expands the directives,
and then reduces what is left to a stream of lexemes for
.bd
c0
to parse.  The compiler is spared reading characters twice, which on
this machine is worth having.

Given "-o base", the lexeme stream is written to "base.x".  That is
what
.bd
c0
reads.  With -p a readable form of the same thing is written to
"base.i" as well.

It handles #define with and without arguments, #undef, #include,
#if, #ifdef, #ifndef, #else, #elif, #endif and #line, and the usual
predefined names.  Conditional arithmetic is done at the width the
target uses, not the host's, so an expression tests the same way in
the compiler as it will in the program.

The options are:

.in +10
.ta 10
.ti -10
-o base	Write the lexeme stream to "base.x".  Required unless -E
was given.

.ti -10
-I dir	Add
.bd
dir
to the list searched for files named in quotes.  These are searched
before the system directory.

.ti -10
-i dir	Set the directory searched for files named in angle brackets.

.ti -10
-Dname	Define
.bd
name,
as though the source began with a #define.  With no value it is
defined as 1.

.ti -10
-E	Preprocess and write the result to standard output rather
than to a lexeme file.

.ti -10
-p	Write "base.i" beside "base.x": the same output in a form
that can be read.

.ti -10
-N	Suppress the line markers that tell the later passes which
line of which file a lexeme came from.  Diagnostics from
.bd
c0
and
.bd
c1
lose their positions, so this is for looking at output, not for
building.

.ti -10
-h	Print a summary of these options.

.ti -10
-v mask	Set the debugging verbosity to the hexadecimal
.bd
mask.
Present only in a compiler built with debugging.

.in -10
.in -5
.bd
